  "summary": "Florida prosecutors dropped charges against former Pinellas County deputy Karl Gwynne, who allegedly ran 381 ALPR searches on a woman who rejected his advances, saying current Florida statutes don't cover his conduct. The State Attorney's Office filed a No Information on Oct. 30 and plans to ask the legislature to amend the law; Sheriff Bob Gualtieri said charges could still be refiled.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Karl Gwynne, 57, used the agency's automated license plate reader database to run 381 searches for one license plate, 207 of which were made while off duty.",
      "paragraph_idx": 1
    },
    {
      "quote": "the current Florida Statutory provisions do not address Gwynne's reported conduct",
      "paragraph_idx": 3
    },
    {
      "quote": "the state attorney's office will seek to have the legislature \"correct this situation\" in the next legislative session",
      "paragraph_idx": 4
    }
  ],
